{:now [{:claim "No list of two-letter sequences is complete: the flipped diagonal is in none of the rows"
        :modern "There is no surjection from the natural numbers onto the set of functions from N to {0, 1}; equivalently the set 2^N is uncountable."
        :tex "\\neg\\,\\exists f:\\mathbb{N}\\twoheadrightarrow\\{0,1\\}^{\\mathbb{N}}"}
       {:claim "For every set L there is a set of stronger power (the powers have no maximum)"
        :modern "Cantor's theorem: for every set A there is no surjection from A onto its power set P(A), hence |A| < |P(A)|; the witness is D = {x in A : x not in f(x)}, valid for infinite A as well, in ZF without choice."
        :tex "|A| < |\\mathcal{P}(A)|,\\qquad D=\\{x\\in A : x\\notin f(x)\\}"}
       {:claim "The powers are the actually infinite cardinal numbers"
        :modern "Beth numbers: beth_0 = |N|, beth_{n+1} = 2^{beth_n}, an unbounded increasing sequence of infinite cardinals; whether beth_1 is aleph_1 is the continuum hypothesis, independent of ZFC (Goedel 1940, Cohen 1963)."
        :tex "\\beth_{n+1}=2^{\\beth_n}"}]
 :fields [{:field :set-theory :how "The theorem gives the ladder of infinite cardinals and the power-set axiom its point; it is the first theorem of every set-theory course." :example "|N| < |P(N)| < |P(P(N))| < ..., and the continuum 2^aleph_0 = |R|."}
          {:field :logic-computing :label "Computing" :how "The diagonal is the proof pattern of the halting problem and of Goedel's incompleteness theorem; Lawvere (1969) stated all of them as one fixed-point argument." :example "Turing 1936: no program decides whether every program halts, by flipping the diagonal of the table 'program i halts on input j'."}
          {:field :logic-computing :label "Paradox" :how "Russell's paradox (1901) is D for f(x) = x on the collection of all sets, which is why there is no set of all sets." :example "{x : x not in x} is not a set."}
          {:field :analysis :how "Uncountability of the reals by a diagonal on decimal expansions, and the existence of transcendental numbers without constructing one." :example "Almost every real number is transcendental: the algebraic numbers are countable."}]
 :tangents [{:series :set-theory :hook "The set-theory course, after Pinter's A Book of Set Theory, takes Cantor's theorem in its chapters on cardinal numbers." :link "/set-theory/"}
            {:series :proof-and-machine :hook "The same flip of a diagonal builds Goedel's unprovable sentence and Turing's non-halting machine."}]
